# Defect 462, 2026-10-07. Symptom: a `str` read after its last release went
# on reading the freed block: a string of 65,536 bytes whose every reference
# C released answered its length and a slice from freed memory, printing
# `65536` and `abababab`, and panel 190's control, one release written twice
# in this tree's C, printed five NUL bytes where `p-one` had been, at `-O0`
# and `-O2`; each stopped only at the program's own release (measured on
# this Mac at `56def9b4`). Cause: only a new reference and a release asked
# the block's mark, and every reader asked whether the str was null and no
# more. Now every reader asks the mark too (`hero_str_require`,
# `runtime/parts/str.c`), and the runtime overwrites the mark before a block
# goes, so the first read after the last release stops there by name.
#
# The string is 65,536 bytes, the size defect 314's case chose: under the
# size glibc gives back to the system at once (a read of an unmapped block
# would be a fault, not this check's to name). A C function of the
# program's own releases every reference to it, and the length is the first
# read after. AddressSanitizer names the read of the freed block.

extern "fixedbugs-462-a-str-read-after-its-last-release-stops-at-the-read.h"
    function release_all(s: cstr lent)

function main()
    word = repeat("ab", 32768)
    print("given back")
    release_all(s: word.cstr())
    print(word.len())
    print(word.slice(from: 0, to: 8))
